Time-based Inference Attack
Attack Principle: When application has completely no differential response, judge condition truth/false through database response delay
Feature Detection:
Basic delay functions:
SLEEP(n): direct delay of n seconds
BENCHMARK(count, expr): repeatedly execute expression
WAIT FOR DELAY '00:00:05' (SQL Server)
pg_sleep(n) (PostgreSQL)
Conditional delay structures:
IF(condition, SLEEP(5), 0)
CASE WHEN condition THEN SLEEP(5) ELSE 0 END
AND IF(condition, SLEEP(5), 0)
Complex delay implementations:
Cartesian product: (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM table1, table2, table3)
Regex backtracking: RLIKE 'a.*a.*a.*a.*a.*a.*a.*a.*b' with long strings
Super long string operations: REPEAT('a', 10000000), RPAD('a', 10000000, 'b')
Nested subqueries: multi-layer nested SELECTs causing performance issues
Recursive calculations: WITH RECURSIVE constructing time-consuming recursion
Heavy functions: MD5(REPEAT('a',1000000))
Delay combined with conditional judgment:
Delay functions appearing in conditional expressions
Whether to execute delay based on certain conditions
Judgment Points:
Whether deliberate delay operations exist
Whether delay is combined with conditional judgment
Whether attempting to infer information through time differences